INT. FAMILY ATTIC - LATE AFTERNOON

Slanted rafters. Dust motes. A circular window throws a pale coin of light on boxes, a dress form, and an old trunk with brass corners.

HENRIK LUND, 70, in a cardigan, sits on the trunk with a cardboard box in his lap. His daughter SIGRID, 40, stands by the window with a roll of packing tape and a marker.

HENRIK
This one says "Christmas."

SIGRID
Then keep it.

HENRIK
It's got one ornament in it.

SIGRID
Then it's the right box.

HENRIK
Your mother wrapped them in newspaper. It's from 1979.

SIGRID
Dad.

HENRIK
It's a very nice paper.

SIGRID
Dad, we have to be out by Friday.

HENRIK
I know the date.

SIGRID
The buyers are bringing a truck.

HENRIK
I know about the truck.

SIGRID
Then what are you doing?

HENRIK
I'm reading the newspaper.

SIGRID
It's forty years old.

HENRIK
Yes. It says the harbor froze. There's a photo of your uncle on the ice.

SIGRID
Uncle Jonas?

HENRIK
With a fish as long as his arm.

SIGRID
He lied about that fish.

HENRIK
He lied about all of them.

SIGRID
You could take the photo.

HENRIK
I could.

SIGRID
You could take the box.

HENRIK
I could take the ornament.

SIGRID
That's the idea.

HENRIK
Sigrid.

SIGRID
Yes.

HENRIK
When I'm gone, who will remember where the box was?

SIGRID
Me.

HENRIK
You won't.

SIGRID
I will.

HENRIK
You'll be in another country.

SIGRID
Then I'll remember it from there.

HENRIK
That's not the same.

SIGRID
No. It's not.

HENRIK
But it's what we have.

SIGRID
It's what we have.

HENRIK
Hand me the tape.

SIGRID
You're keeping it?

HENRIK
I'm closing it. You said I could keep it.

SIGRID
I did.

HENRIK
Then keep the tape.

She hands him the roll. He seals the box, slowly, the tape shrieking, and she writes on the lid in marker: HENRIK, LAST BOX.
